✅ 7. Set Thread Priority and Observe Behavior

7.1 Definition
🔴 Every Java thread has a priority.
🔴 Priority is an integer value ranging from 1 to 10.
🔴 It is a hint to the JVM and Operating System scheduler about the relative importance of a thread.
🔴 The scheduler may use this information while deciding which thread to execute next.

7.2 Priority Range

| Constant               |  Value | Description      |
| ---------------------- | -----: | ---------------- |
| `Thread.MIN_PRIORITY`  |   1    | Lowest priority  |
| `Thread.NORM_PRIORITY` |   5    | Default priority |
| `Thread.MAX_PRIORITY`  |   10   | Highest priority |

7.3 Default Priority
Every new thread inherits the priority of the thread that creates it.

Example:
Thread t = new Thread();

By default:
Priority = 5

Since the main thread starts with priority 5, threads created by it also have priority 5 unless explicitly changed.

7.4 Methods Used

Set Priority
🔴 thread.setPriority(int priority);
Get Priority
🔴 thread.getPriority();

Constants

🔴 Thread.MIN_PRIORITY
🔴 Thread.NORM_PRIORITY
🔴 Thread.MAX_PRIORITY

7.5 Syntax

Thread t = new Thread(task);
t.setPriority(Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);
t.start();

7.6 How Priority Works Internally

Suppose:
Thread-A → Priority 10
Thread-B → Priority 5
Thread-C → Priority 1

Flow:

Runnable Threads
        │
        ▼
Thread Scheduler
        │
 ┌──────┼────────┐
 ▼      ▼        ▼
A(10)  B(5)    C(1)

The scheduler may prefer Thread-A.

But it can also execute:

Thread-B
Thread-C

Priority is only a scheduling hint.

7.7 Does Higher Priority Guarantee Execution First?
❌ No.

This is one of the most common interview traps.

Even if:

t1.setPriority(10);
t2.setPriority(1);

Output could still be:

Thread-2
Thread-1

because scheduling depends on:

🔴 JVM implementation
🔴 Operating System scheduler
🔴 CPU availability
🔴 Thread state
🔴 System load
